# `M154g` / `C75` — the known-answer plant for `with each`. **This file is meant to pass.**
#
# `with each` is used across this suite and its uses do demonstrate that *something* iterates. What
# none of them can state is the manifest's actual claim, which is about **reporting**: *"run one
# copy of the test per row, each reporting as its own test"*. A table whose rows were run in a loop
# inside a single reported test would satisfy every existing assertion — the requests all go out,
# the expectations all hold, and the summary says one more test passed instead of three.
#
# So the row's value is spent on a label the server counts, and the grader reads two surfaces that
# disagree under that wrong implementation: `report/results.json` must carry **three** tests from
# this file with three distinct interpolated names, and the arrival counter must hold three distinct
# labels at one each.
#
# `expect body.count equals 1` is the in-band half of the same claim: a row executed twice would
# answer 2 here without changing the number of reported tests at all.

before file
  api POST /lifecycle/reset
  expect status equals 200

@constructs @datatables
with each
  | row     |
  | "alpha" |
  | "beta"  |
  | "gamma" |
test "one reported test per row — this is row {row}"
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c75{row}" }
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.label equals "c75{row}"
  expect body.count equals 1
